The == operator in Java compares the references of the objects, not the actual values. This means that two strings with the same content will not be considered equal if they are stored in different memory locations. The .equals() method, on the other hand, compares the actual values of the strings. 

To compare strings in Java effectively, you should use the .equals() method, especially when dealing with user-input strings or any strings that may have the same content but different references. Here's how you can use it:

```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("Strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("Strings are not equal");
}
```

In this example, `str1` and `str2` will be considered equal because they have the same content.

The == operator can be useful in certain situations, such as when comparing primitive data types (like int, char, etc.) or when you are certain that two references point to the exact same object. However, for most string comparisons, using .equals() is the recommended approach.

